<% content_for :title, "Privacy — Openstage" %>
<% content_for :main_class, "os-wrap os-stack pt-1.5" %>
<% content_for :meta_description, "What Openstage counts on builders' websites and its own pages, what it reads from Stripe, and what it keeps." %>

<section class="os-panel p-6 sm:p-12">
  <span class="os-tag">Privacy</span>
  <h1 class="os-h1 mt-4 max-w-[900px]">What Openstage counts, and what it keeps.</h1>
  <p class="os-p mt-4.5 max-w-[720px] text-lg">Openstage counts visits so builders can see who reads their work. It counts people in totals, never as profiles.</p>
  <p class="os-xs os-muted mt-3">Updated November 9, 2026.</p>
</section>

<section id="builders-sites" class="os-panel scroll-mt-6" aria-labelledby="on-builders-sites">
  <h2 id="on-builders-sites" class="os-h2">On a builder's website</h2>
  <div class="mt-3 max-w-[720px] space-y-3.5">
    <p class="os-p">
      A builder can add Openstage's script to their project's website to count its visits. On each page you view there, it
      sends Openstage the page's address and the project's code. When a page loads, it also sends the address of the page
      you came from. The request carries your IP address and browser name, as every request does. It sets no cookie and
      stores nothing in your browser.
    </p>
    <p class="os-p">
      Openstage adds the view to that day's totals for the project: page views, and visitors, a visitor being one IP
      address, or one IPv6 network, on one day. It counts each page by its path, without the query string, and replaces
      with an asterisk any part of the path that looks like an email address, and most that look like a long id or
      token. Once a day, it also counts the name of the site you came from, the ref or utm_source tag in the page's
      address, and your country as Cloudflare reports it. To count you once a day, it keeps a hash of your IP address
      made with a key that changes every day, and deletes it within three days. For an IPv6 address, it hashes only the
      first half, the part that names your network. It does not keep your IP address or your device.
    </p>
    <p class="os-p">
      Openstage does not count views from bots, from sites other than the project's own, or on a project's site while its
      counting is off or paused. The builder sees these totals, and the builder's stage and the project's page on Openstage
      can show the project's page views and visitors.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="os-panel" aria-labelledby="on-openstage">
  <h2 id="on-openstage" class="os-h2">On Openstage's own pages</h2>
  <div class="mt-3 max-w-[720px] space-y-3.5">
    <p class="os-p">
      A visit to a builder's stage or a project's page records the time, the site you came from, your country as
      Cloudflare reports it, and a hash of your IP address with the date, not the address itself. The hash is made with
      a secret key, so without that key it cannot be turned back into the address. Openstage deletes it within three days and
      keeps a random ID in its place, which says only which of that day's visits came from the same visitor. A click on a
      page's links records the link and the time. Bots and the builder's own visits are left out. Builders on Pro see these numbers.
    </p>
    <p class="os-p">Openstage's request log leaves out your IP address, on its own pages and for the script's page views.</p>
    <p class="os-p">Openstage's own site analytics run on Umami, which sets no cookie, and on Openstage's script, which counts this site as it counts a builder's website.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="os-panel" aria-labelledby="if-you-sign-in">
  <h2 id="if-you-sign-in" class="os-h2">If you sign in, follow or pay</h2>
  <div class="mt-3 max-w-[720px] space-y-3.5">
    <p class="os-p">
      Signing in with GitHub gives Openstage your GitHub username, name, avatar and email, and a token to read your
      repositories' commits, which it stores encrypted. Deleting your account in Settings removes it for good after 30 days.
      Openstage also saves your browser's time zone to count your days in it, and you can change it in Settings.
    </p>
    <p class="os-p">
      Following a builder or a project keeps your email. Nothing but the confirmation is sent until you confirm, and
      every email after it has a one-click unsubscribe.
    </p>
    <p class="os-p">Payments go through Stripe; Openstage never sees your card.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="stripe" class="os-panel scroll-mt-6" aria-labelledby="if-you-connect-stripe">
  <h2 id="if-you-connect-stripe" class="os-h2">If you connect Stripe</h2>
  <div class="mt-3 max-w-[720px] space-y-3.5">
    <p class="os-p">
      A builder can connect Stripe to show a project's MRR. Openstage takes only a restricted key, refuses one that could
      also charge or refund, and stores it encrypted. Once a day, it uses the key to read the account's products, prices,
      subscriptions and invoices.
    </p>
    <p class="os-p">
      From those it works out each product's MRR and paying subscriptions for each day, and how many customers paid for
      which products. It keeps those daily totals, and
      each product's name, Stripe id and whether it is archived. Stripe's answers can carry a customer's name and email,
      and Openstage stores neither: no invoice, no subscription and no customer's record is kept.
    </p>
    <p class="os-p">
      The builder sees these totals. On Pro, the project's page and the builder's stage show them to visitors too, unless
      the builder hides them; on Free, only the builder sees them. To write them in one currency, Openstage uses the
      European Central Bank's published rate for each day, and asking for those rates sends the bank nothing about anyone.
      Disconnecting Stripe in Settings deletes the key and everything read with it at once.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="os-panel" aria-labelledby="questions">
  <h2 id="questions" class="os-h2">Questions</h2>
